{{also|Potash}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
{{calque|en|nl|potas}}, coined in 1598, equivalent to {{compound|en|pot|ash}}. The literal translation is “[[pot]] [[ash]]”, because it was made by burning wood to ashes in a large pot. First attested in 1648. {{doublet|en|potassium|potassa}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɒt.æʃ/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɑt.æʃ/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Wodencafe-potash.wav|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|æʃ|s=2}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-potash.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|water-soluble part of ash}} The [[water]]-[[soluble]] part of the [[ash]] formed by [[burning]] [[plant]] [[material]]; used for making [[soap]] and [[glass]] and as a [[fertilizer]].
#: {{hyper|en|ash}}
#: {{cot|en|fly ash}}
# {{senseid|en|impure form of potassium carbonate}} {{lb|en|chemistry}} An [[impure]] form of [[potassium]] [[carbonate]] (K<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>) mixed with other potassium [[salt]]s.
# {{senseid|en|potassium, archaically}} {{lb|en|chemistry|archaic}} {{l|en|potassium|Potassium}}. {{n-g|Chiefly used in the names of [[compound]]s of the form "... of potash".}}
#: {{uxi|en|[[permanganate of potash]]|[[potassium permanganate]]}}
# {{senseid|en|class of potassium minerals}} A class of [[potassium]] minerals, of similar applicability to [[potassium carbonate]], such as being a fertilizer. (ie. [[potassium chloride]], [[potassium hydroxide]])

